    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let's review the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Я не хотела, правда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I didn't mean to, honestly.' Sasha combines 'I' with 'honestly' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Я сейчас все вытру.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will wipe it all up now.' Sasha links 'I' directly with 'now' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не надо плакать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Don't cry.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'not' with 'to cry'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Все хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Everything is fine.' Notice how 'all' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'good'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Вы меня уволите?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Will you fire me?' Sasha uses the polite plural 'you' alongside 'will fire', keeping a formal or respectful register.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Вы расскажете все руководству.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You will tell management everything.' Sasha links 'you' directly with 'will tell' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Никуда я не пойду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am not going anywhere.' Max combines 'nowhere' with 'go' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ничего я не скажу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I won't say anything.' In Russian, 'nothing' leads into 'I'll tell' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Извините меня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Excuse me.' Sasha uses the imperative command form of the verb meaning 'sorry'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Я правда не хотела.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I really didn't mean to.' Sasha links 'I' directly with 'honestly' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Успокойся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Calm down.' Max replies with a single verb form meaning 'calm down' — in Russian, the verb ending alone carries the subject and tense.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
